ing  this development phase um which  is really important when you're thinking  about the checks the scale uh and the  types of change management projects that  a government might be looking at  uh just further not every abstraction  tool should necessarily be considered a  platform uh platform's products and  services all abstract away some part of  the technical infrastructure in some way  uh and platforms tend to have a really  unique uh  sort of feature where you also have  complements that are built on top of  uh that that technical infrastructure  operate within this ecosystem and feed  off each other and so as we're thinking  about building government platforms at  scale it's also really important to  understand um  how platforms can go beyond just  abstraction tools  this uh led to a working definition  of platforms as easy stones  low and hydride components with  specified and stable interfaces through  which one accesses those components this  is a working definition of platforms  that was used in this research  that came from about three dozen  practitioner interviews with folks who  are building a platform in government or  otherwise doing digital government work  uh and a pretty comprehensive literature  review of  relevant topics  uh we've come to this definition uh as  sort of this three-part ecosystem in  which all three of these parts are  equally as important for something to be  considered a platform and if something  doesn't have one of these parts  frequently something might not have uh a  system of high variety components or  complements that operate within this  ecosystem uh it might be an abstraction  tool it might be a really interesting  product that helps governments deliver  services in really interesting ways or  otherwise is quite powerful um but for  the purposes of this research and  thinking through how governments  should be building and operationalizing  platforms it's just one platform  this definition isn't necessarily just  about  what it is  it's designed to give us information and  language that we need uh to have policy  makers and developers work together to  discuss how to build uh the desired  features within these parameters  just to expand on the definition a  little bit to make sure it's really  clear before going into the next part of  the presentation  uh  this definition  sort of at a high level can be matched  to a  component of the  ios operating platform there are low  variety components these highly reusable  elements from ecosystems like ios  um  that sort of  don't change as frequently and are the  core  of the apps and complements both on top  of it there are high variety components  uh sort of the number of components that  are peripheral like applications uh and  there's a stable interface like the app  store through which applications uh can  be marketed on this platform ecosystem  that's what makes the iphone uh sort of  a funny system in a way  that is not true other products and  services  so using uh this definition of platforms  it's interesting to think about how  governments might decide to build a  platforming system and what types of  choices they would need to make to  ensure that they're building  something that's going to meet their end  goal  there are ways to build something that  has high and low variety components and  stable interfaces  uh in a variety of ways that meet not  only the goals of the government but  also the real  and perceived constraints that come with  doing technical work in a government  space  so the things that influence uh how this  how a platform might be built in this  way are  first and primarily uh technical and  policy choices  some of these technical and policy  choices are  should the interface uh be open or  closed uh there are a lot of decisions  that go into  why a government building a platform  ecosystem might want to have  an open or closed interface and that  decision is going to ultimately dictate  how powerful and 