ing  this development phase um which  is really important when you're thinking  about the checks the scale uh and the  types of change management projects that  a government might be looking at  uh just further not every abstraction  tool should necessarily be considered a  platform uh platform's products and  services all abstract away some part of  the technical infrastructure in some way  uh and platforms tend to have a really  unique uh  sort of feature where you also have  complements that are built on top of  uh that that technical infrastructure  operate within this ecosystem and feed  off each other and so as we're thinking  about building government platforms at  scale it's also really important to  understand um  how platforms can go beyond just  abstraction tools  this uh led to a working definition  of platforms as easy stones  low and hydride components with  specified and stable interfaces through  which one accesses those components this  is a working definition of platforms  that was used in this research  that came from about three dozen  practitioner interviews with folks who  are building a platform in government or  otherwise doing digital government work  uh and a pretty comprehensive literature  review of  relevant topics  uh we've come to this definition uh as  sort of this three-part ecosystem in  which all three of these parts are  equally as important for something to be  considered a platform and if something  doesn't have one of these parts  frequently something might not have uh a  system of high variety components or  complements that operate within this  ecosystem uh it might be an abstraction  tool it might be a really interesting  product that helps governments deliver  services in really interesting ways or  otherwise is quite powerful um but for  the purposes of this research and  thinking through how governments  should be building and operationalizing  platforms it's just one platform  this definition isn't necessarily just  abou